  "slug": "nephropathy-iga-43d5",
  "summary": "Spatial readouts now sit alongside single-cell state definitions to link niche composition with injury across kidney, liver, and gut disease.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of single-cell and spatial transcriptomic readouts to define disease-associated cell states and their tissue context. In kidney, a human precision-cut kidney slice platform shows that IFN-gamma induces APOL1 in glomeruli, and a Kidney International study places an oxidative stress-driven STAT1-APOL1 feedforward loop in APOL1-mediated disease, using TRAP-seq and Xenium. In liver and biliary disease, a biliary atresia atlas links expanded neutrophils, cytotoxic lymphocytes and injured endothelia through CXCL12-CXCR4, while autoimmune liver injury is resolved into hepatitis and cholestatic ecotypes. Reviews of gout, IPF/COPD and perianal Crohn's disease synthesize comparable single-cell and spatial evidence, whereas metabolic, cardiovascular and Mendelian randomization items are less directly spatial. What is new is the coupling of spatial tissue readouts to single-cell state definitions within a specified mechanistic axis, here interferon-driven APOL1 induction.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-21"
}
